## Specification Under Review
# Herdr orchestrator operational corrections

Status: council-refined design pending final council document review and user approval.

## Purpose

Make a Meta-O feature run a visibly managed process over persistent specialist sessions. The orchestrator is a transport and lifecycle controller. It does not read the task, specification, business framing, source, tests, review findings, or other tracked-file contents, and it does not form engineering opinions.

For Herdr, the result is directly observable:

- the executor is an ordinary interactive Claude or Codex CLI in the right pane beside the orchestrator;
- two ordinary interactive reviewer CLIs occupy the two right-split panes of a separate review tab;
- the same executor and reviewer panes stay warm across every round of one uninterrupted feature run;
- every prompt is submitted through Herdr’s agent surface, including Enter;
- review output is copied verbatim to the executor only after both independent first passes finish;
- waits follow actor lifecycle, never a predicted Git change;
- every passing gate names the same full Git object ID.

Omnigent preserves the process-only role firewall and the same candidate and gate semantics through its own native agent model. Herdr tabs, panes, TUI extraction, and Herdr commands never enter `mo-omnigent`.

## Scope

This change updates the existing Meta-O skills and knowledge needed for:

- the backend-neutral orchestrator/executor/reviewer role firewall;
- `mo-herdr` topology, actor startup, prompting, waiting, compact handoff retrieval, review transport, and E2E dispatch;
- `mo-omnigent` only where its current behavior violates the backend-neutral firewall or model-catalogue contract;
- `mo-review` and `mo-e2e` handoffs;
- the bundled model-catalogue helper;
- project setup instructions, including version control, backlog, glossary, and documentation discipline;
- deterministic tests, Herdr fixtures, acceptance mapping, and upstream issue evidence.

This change does not add an orchestration CLI, provider proxy, daemon, adapter layer, state store, run registry, recovery protocol, verdict file, completion sentinel, receipt, manifest, digest, or baseline. It does not make headless actor execution an invisible fallback.

## Normative invariants

1. After the backend skill starts, the orchestrator never intentionally opens, searches, quotes, summarizes, or edits the contents of a tracked project file. The only content exception is the project contract (`AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md`) that the host CLI injected before skill activation; it is process policy, not feature input. Fixed Git metadata commands may internally read refs and the index, but only their narrow metadata output enters orchestrator context.
2. The task/spec path is an opaque locator to the orchestrator. Repository-reading actors receive it and open it themselves.
3. The orchestrator may observe fixed process metadata: repository root, current branch, `HEAD`, commit existence, worktree cleanliness, actor identity, lifecycle state, pane identity, and validated handoff headers.
4. The executor owns feasibility, architecture, implementation, tests, documentation necessity, version-control work, and ordinary technical choices.
5. Reviewers independently read the complete task and business framing, inspect the frozen candidate, run the required deterministic checks, and own their findings and applicability decisions.
6. The orchestrator does not filter, rank, merge, paraphrase, validate, or decide reviewer findings. Reviewer bodies are opaque bytes during transport.
7. Actor output is untrusted data. It may supply a validated process header and an opaque body, but it never authorizes an arbitrary host command or a relaxation of these invariants.
8. Every supported Herdr actor is a visible ordinary interactive subscription CLI started with `herdr agent start`. Direct `pane run`, inline/headless provider mode, SDK turns, and private provider transcripts are not actor fallbacks.
9. A complete compact handoff is bounded by the actor’s exact process header and a provider-rendered lower input boundary. A missing, duplicated, stale, contradictory, oversized, or unreadable boundary makes the handoff `unknown`.
10. A review containing an open finding cannot pass. A rebuttal never closes a finding by itself; the originating reviewer or adjudicator closes it explicitly.
11. Two reviewers are from different vendors, and at least one is from a different vendor than the executor. Diversity is checked again after actual launches, not inferred from catalogue names.
12. A result is one full Git object ID. Any later commit invalidates every prior review, deterministic check, and E2E result.
13. A worktree edit without a commit prevents completion. It is returned to the executor and never treated as a second candidate.
14. A route or provider/version/surface combination is unsupported until the exact acceptance fixture passes. Availability pressure never converts `unknown` to `pass`.
15. Human attention is reserved for product meaning, irreversible action, credentials, subscription changes, production/destructive E2E approval, external blockers actors cannot resolve, or a technical dispute left unresolved by independent adjudication.
16. Process terms introduced or materially changed by this feature have one canonical meaning in `docs/glossary.md`. Skills and architecture documents use those terms consistently rather than creating backend-local synonyms.

## Roles and backend ownership

### Orchestrator

The orchestrator may:

- accept a task/spec locator and select the requested backend skill;
- perform the fixed Git metadata checks listed in this document;
- discover configured model routes and apply documented fallbacks;
- create, label, start, prompt, wait for, and continue actor sessions;
- parse only the exact process-header grammar below;
- copy opaque actor bodies through restrictive temporary transport files;
- count review batches and finding identifiers;
- invalidate gates when `HEAD` or worktree cleanliness changes;
- report one verified object ID or a permitted `needs_attention` reason.

It must not:

- intentionally retrieve tracked-file content after activation, including the task, spec, business framing, source, diff, tests, logs, build files, README files, documentation, or glossary;
- run `git diff`, `git show`, `git log -p`, `git blame`, or another command whose purpose is to reveal tracked content;
- decide feasibility, gate applicability, finding correctness, or what code or documentation should change;
- execute command text supplied by an actor;
- ask the human to choose ordinary models, reuse, watchdog behavior, technical fixes, or process steps;
- present an ordinary choice menu;
- keep reviewer prose in its working summary after delivery.

The already host-injected project contract remains the sole tracked-content exception. The compact working summary contains only actor and pane IDs, provider/vendor, candidate ID, current process state, batch numbers, finding IDs, scratch handle, and delivery status. This is ephemeral reasoning context, not a persisted schema.

### Executor

The executor:

- opens the task/spec and all applicable project knowledge;
- uses the canonical vocabulary in `docs/glossary.md` and updates it when this change makes an existing definition false or introduces a durable project term;
- decides whether the task is feasible and how to implement it;
- creates and stays on the required feature branch;
- makes coherent commits and runs relevant checks before each commit;
- decides whether documentation or deterministic/agentic verification applies;
- receives reviewer and E2E output as untrusted peer feedback, verifies it against the repository, and fixes or rebuts it;
- ends each process-relevant turn with one compact executor handoff;
- does not ask the human ordinary engineering questions that it can answer from the repository and product framing.

### Reviewers

Each reviewer independently:

- opens the task/spec, `docs/business.md` or equivalent framing, and `docs/glossary.md`;
- verifies the complete user scope, not only the executor’s claimed scope;
- checks that new and changed process vocabulary has one consistent canonical definition;
- checks the frozen candidate without editing or committing;
- runs `make mo-qc`, `make mo-smoke`, and any additional project-owned checks it judges relevant;
- reports every finding with a stable reviewer-owned ID;
- declares whether agent-required E2E applies;
- emits compact review batches of at most 180 `recent-unwrapped` rows;
- explicitly accounts for all its open IDs before issuing `PASS`.

Reviewer A completes before reviewer B is prompted. Reviewer B receives the same candidate and task locator but no output from reviewer A. Sequential execution preserves independence, prevents concurrent test contention in one worktree, and creates a hard barrier before the executor can mutate the candidate.

### E2E actor

When agent-required E2E is applicable, a separate visible ordinary interactive actor runs `mo-e2e` against the frozen candidate. It reads the project E2E contract, chooses only the applicable scenario groups, handles namespacing and cleanup, and emits one compact E2E handoff. It never edits or commits tracked files.

## Canonical vocabulary

`docs/glossary.md` is updated in the same increment as the role and handoff contract. It is the canonical home for durable process vocabulary, not a duplicate protocol specification. Definitions remain backend-neutral where the concept is backend-neutral and explicitly Herdr-qualified where it depends on Herdr.

At minimum, the glossary defines or reconciles:

- **actor** — a visible, persistent specialist CLI session participating in one run;
- **orchestrator** — the process-only transport and lifecycle controller;
- **executor** — the repository-reading actor that owns implementation and commits;
- **reviewer** — an independent, read-only evaluator of one frozen candidate;
- **E2E actor** — the separate actor that performs agent-required E2E;
- **feature run** — one uninterrupted orchestration attempt; restart begins a new run;
- **candidate** — the full Git object ID at clean `HEAD` presented for verification;
- **candidate freeze** — the period in which candidate `HEAD` and cleanliness must remain unchanged;
- **gate** — candidate-bound evidence whose validity is lost after any new commit;
- **verified result** — the one unchanged full Git object ID that passed every applicable gate;
- **compact handoff** — a bounded process header plus an opaque body extracted from one completed actor turn;
- **process header** — the exact validated first line used only for mechanical routing and accounting;
- **opaque body** — actor-authored bytes transported without semantic inspection by the orchestrator;
- **provider lower boundary** — the fixture-proven rendered input neighborhood that bounds the completed handoff from below;
- **first-pass barrier** — the point after complete independent first passes from both reviewers, before review bodies may reach the executor;
- **finding** — a reviewer-owned issue identified by a stable `A-*` or `B-*` ID;
- **adjudication** — one other-vendor decision on a disputed finding, which does not itself make the originating review pass;
- **lifecycle state** — Herdr’s direct observation of actor state, distinct from terminal prose or repository changes;
- **route** — a backend/provider/version/surface combination supported only by its exact fixtures;
- **catalogue availability**, **model presence**, **launchability**, and **entitlement** — distinct facts represented respectively by enumeration success, configured-ID presence, readiness after launch, and actual usable subscription access;
- **`unknown`** — incomplete or unreadable gate evidence that cannot be treated as partial success;
- **`needs_attention`** — a terminal orchestration outcome requiring a permitted human boundary, not a review verdict;
- **warm session** — the same actor and pane retained across turns within one uninterrupted run;
- **scratch transport** — per-run restrictive storage outside the repository for opaque handoff bytes.

The glossary does not reproduce header field order, retry tables, command syntax, row limits, or provider fixtures; those remain in their normative architecture, skill, and acceptance homes. Existing definitions are edited instead of adding competing entries. Deterministic tests verify required terms, unique definitions, and consistent use of `candidate`, `gate`, `unknown`, `needs_attention`, `compact handoff`, and `feature run` across the relevant source skills and knowledge files.

## Herdr operational interface

### Preconditions

`mo-herdr` requires `HERDR_ENV=1`, a real repository, an interactive orchestrator pane, the public Herdr commands named here, and at least two actually launchable reviewer vendors whose exact TUI fixtures pass. Failed preconditions are reported without a headless substitute.

The orchestrator may use these fixed Git metadata commands and no content-bearing variants:

```text
git rev-parse --show-toplevel
git rev-parse HEAD
git branch --show-current
git status --porcelain
git cat-file -e <validated-object-id>^{commit}
```

An object ID is validated as the complete output of `git rev-parse`; the contract does not assume SHA-1 or a fixed hexadecimal width. Actor strings never become shell syntax.

### Visible topology

Let `<slug>` be the lowercase basename of the opaque task locator, replace every non-alphanumeric run with `-`, trim separators, and keep at most 12 characters. If empty, use `task`. Actor names are `m-<slug>-<executor|reviewera|reviewerb|e2e>-<suffix>`, where `<suffix>` is six lowercase alphanumeric characters derived from the current orchestrator pane.

This reserves enough space for the longest role under Herdr’s 32-character limit, always starts with a letter, and has no recovery meaning. Validate completed names against `[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}` and uniqueness before mutating topology; a collision gets a fresh current-run suffix.

```text
tab "mo:<slug>"
├── orchestrator       left, existing pane
└── executor           right, vertical split

tab "mo:<slug>:review"
├── reviewer A         left, root pane
└── reviewer B         right, vertical split
```

An applicable E2E actor is created lazily in a visible tab `mo:<slug>:e2e`; no fixed multi-pane E2E layout is required.

Construction uses `pane split --direction right --no-focus`, tab creation/move, and `herdr tab rename <TAB_ID> <LABEL>` from the installed public CLI. It does not steal focus, close user panes, replace vertical splits with horizontal ones, or hide roles. Tab labels remain after the run for observability; there is no restore step. Rename failure is retried once and then becomes `needs_attention`.

### Actor startup and model arguments

Every role is started in an existing shell pane:

```text
herdr agent start <name> --kind claude --pane <id> -- --model <id> --effort <level>
herdr agent start <name> --kind codex  --pane <id> -- --model <id> --config model_reasoning_effort=<level>
```

These forms are supported only after provider/version fixtures prove that the selected model is active. Other Herdr kinds remain unsupported until their exact catalogue, argument, lifecycle, and extraction fixtures exist.

`agent start` is the normal route because it resolves the system CLI, verifies the expected interactive process in the pane, names the actor, and exposes lifecycle. Directly running `claude` or `codex` is diagnostic only.

A catalogue-listed model whose CLI exits before Herdr readiness is `launch_failed`, not `model_missing`. A ready actor proves launchability for that run. Fallback launches are checked again for real vendor diversity.

### Atomic prompting

All ordinary work uses one call:

```text
herdr agent prompt <actor> <text> --wait --until idle --until done \
  --until blocked --until unknown --timeout <milliseconds>
```

The actor must be settled before submission. `agent prompt` provides text and Enter atomically; `pane send-text`, separate `send-keys enter`, terminal typing, and shell-injected provider commands are forbidden for ordinary prompts.

Recommended user start:

```text
/goal Run mo-herdr for <TASK_OR_SPEC_PATH>.
```

Executor goal:

```text
/goal Implement <TASK_OR_SPEC_PATH> to a verified candidate; own repository reading, decisions, branch, checks, commits, and compact Meta-O handoffs without asking ordinary technical questions.
```

Reviewer and E2E prompts similarly name the task locator, candidate, role, compact handoff version, and maximum batch size without embedding tracked-file contents.

## Compact handoff protocol

### Why a process header is allowed

The first line is process metadata, not a completion sentinel. Completion is proven by the provider-rendered lower input boundary after Herdr observes the actor settle. The header only marks where the opaque payload begins and provides fields the process-only orchestrator may route.

No Markdown structure is parsed. Headings, fences, verdict prose, code blocks, and finding bodies are opaque. Simple exact-line and delimiter processing is limited to the ASCII grammar below.

### Grammar

Every header is one physical `recent-unwrapped` line:

```text
MO_EXECUTOR_V1|type=<READY|CANDIDATE|RESPONSE|BLOCKER>|candidate=<oid|none>|branch=<name|none>|base=<oid|none>|fixes=<ids|none>|rebuts=<ids|none>|blocker=<class|none>
MO_REVIEW_V1|candidate=<oid>|reviewer=<A|B>|status=<PASS|FINDINGS|DISPUTED|UNKNOWN>|batch=<positive-int>|more=<yes|no>|ids=<ids|none>|open=<ids|none>|closes=<ids|none>|qc=<PASS|FAIL|UNKNOWN>|smoke=<PASS|FAIL|UNKNOWN>|e2e=<REQUIRED|NA|UNKNOWN>|unknown=<transport|environment|evaluation|none>
MO_ADJUDICATION_V1|candidate=<oid>|finding=<id>|reviewer=<A|B>|outcome=<UPHOLD|WITHDRAW|UNRESOLVED>
MO_E2E_V1|candidate=<oid>|status=<PASS|FAIL|UNKNOWN>|scenarios=<positive-int>|not_run=<none|positive-int>
```

Fields appear exactly once in the shown order. Values use restricted character sets; finding IDs are `A-<positive-int>` or `B-<positive-int>` and comma-separated without spaces. Unknown fields, duplicates, malformed values, candidate mismatch, reviewer mismatch, non-monotonic batches, and contradictory values make the handoff `unknown`.

The body begins on the next row and continues to the provider lower boundary. Executor, review, and E2E bodies are limited to 180 `recent-unwrapped` rows and 64 KiB of UTF-8 including the header. A larger result is split into numbered batches; `more=yes` prompts the same warm actor for the next compact batch. An over-byte batch is not sliced: the actor is asked once to reissue it as smaller semantic batches, then the handoff is `unknown`.

`PASS` requires `more=no`, `open=none`, `ids=none`, `qc=PASS`, `smoke=PASS`, `unknown=none`, and `e2e` equal to `REQUIRED` or `NA`. `FINDINGS` names every finding introduced in that batch in `ids`. A later `PASS` lists every previously open ID in `closes`. An `UNKNOWN` header names exactly one non-`none` unknown class.

Across all batches for one actor turn, `candidate`, `reviewer`, `status`, `qc`, `smoke`, `e2e`, and `unknown` are identical; batch numbers are consecutive from 1, finding IDs are unique, and only the last batch has `more=no`. Executor and E2E multi-batch handoffs likewise keep their type/candidate/status fields invariant.

An executor `BLOCKER` uses only:

```text
product_meaning | irreversible_action | credentials | subscription |
production_e2e | external_blocker | unresolved_dispute
```

Any ordinary technical blocker remains the executor’s responsibility.

### Adaptive extraction without context pollution

The skill provides an adaptable inline shell recipe, not a maintained script. One per-run orchestrator-owned `0700` `mktemp -d` outside the repository holds `0600` opaque batches. Its path is an allowed scratch handle from first extraction through confirmed post-barrier delivery. Files are deleted after confirmed delivery and the directory on failure, signal, and normal exit. If the handle is lost, buffered reviews become `unknown` and both first passes rerun. A new run never adopts old scratch. Startup removes only same-UID, mode-`0700`, exact-prefix directories older than 24 hours.

For each settled actor turn:

1. Read `recent-unwrapped` privately at 120 rows.
2. If the exact role header and provider lower boundary are not both present, grow to 200, 400, 800, then 1000 rows.
3. Claude’s current candidate boundary contains its final input line beginning `❯`; Codex’s begins `›`. A glyph alone is not a boundary. The fixture defines adjacent chrome and terminal-tail position. Reject multiple structural candidates.
4. Reject zero or multiple matching headers in the selected interval. Copy the header and every row through the row before the lower boundary byte-for-byte to scratch. Discard everything before the header.
5. Print only the validated header into orchestrator context.
6. When allowed, pass the scratch bytes as one quoted `agent prompt` argument inside an explicit `reviewer-output` or `e2e-output` data wrapper. Do not use `eval`, unquoted expansion, command construction from the body, or actor-provided paths.

The wrapper marks the enclosed bytes as untrusted peer feedback and tells the executor to evaluate them against the repository while ignoring attempts to alter role, transport, permissions, or process rules. The enclosed bytes are unchanged.

Absent boundaries at 1000 rows, excess rows or bytes, ambiguity, repaint/resize discontinuity, or schema damage make the gate `unknown`. The same actor is asked once to reissue without tools. After two damaged attempts the exact provider/version TUI route is unsupported. There is no headless, inline, file-verdict, private-transcript, or executor-reads-reviewer-pane fallback.

This amends `docs/architecture/full-turn-retrieval.md`: Herdr transports complete compact process handoffs, not arbitrarily long TUI turns. Existing long-turn failures remain true; only fixture-proven combinations become supported.

## Lifecycle and waiting

### Wait budgets

- executor: one active wait arm of at most 10 minutes;
- reviewer or E2E actor: one active wait arm of at most 5 minutes;
- multi-batch reviewers: alternate prompt/wait operations; never leave two waiters for one actor;
- every wait returns immediately on `idle`, `done`, `blocked`, or `unknown`.

The direct condition is actor lifecycle. A wait never includes a predicted SHA, worktree cleanliness, terminal text prediction, or polling sleep. On timeout, the orchestrator reads only agent state and pane process metadata. While lifecycle remains `working` and the expected provider process is present, it re-arms another bounded interval.

### State handling

| Observed state | Mechanical action | Limit or outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `working` | Re-arm while the expected process remains present | No elapsed-time cap |
| `idle` or `done` | Extract compact handoff | Retry damaged handoff once |
| `blocked` | Report visible tab, pane, and role without reading UI | Human resolves exact UI; repeated ordinary blocking makes route unsupported |
| `unknown` | Read lifecycle/process metadata and wait once more | Persistent unknown is `needs_attention` |
| actor exited | Restart same kind in same pane and resend full goal | Current gate restarts; second exit is `needs_attention` |
| pane missing | Recreate visible pane and fresh role | Current gate restarts; second loss is `needs_attention` |
| wrong UI/not ready | Try next supported route once | Recheck vendor diversity |

The visible pane is the narrow blocker channel. The user reads and answers the exact provider UI; the orchestrator reports only location, role, and state. Provider posture must prove ordinary repository trust and reversible tool use do not routinely stop the route in a blocked UI.

### Orchestrator restart

There is no restoration protocol. A restarted orchestrator performs ordinary startup from current repository state, creates new panels and actors, and sends full role goals. It does not adopt, interrogate, or close sessions from the previous run. Old visible panes remain untouched. Warm-session continuity exists only within one uninterrupted feature run.

## Candidate, review, gate, and E2E flow

```text
preflight
  -> executor_active
  -> candidate_frozen
  -> reviewer_A
  -> reviewer_B
  -> first_pass_barrier
       -> resolution -> executor_active
       -> e2e
       -> verified
  -> e2e
       -> resolution -> executor_active
       -> verified
       -> needs_attention
```

### Candidate freeze

A `CANDIDATE` handoff is accepted only when:

- `git status --porcelain` is empty;
- declared candidate equals `git rev-parse HEAD`;
- `git cat-file -e <candidate>^{commit}` succeeds;
- current branch equals the declared branch and matches `feature/<short-slug>`;
- the executor declares the full current `develop` base it used.

The orchestrator does not decide project-specific ancestry policy. Both reviewers validate the claim. Before and after each reviewer and E2E turn, the orchestrator repeats only `HEAD` and cleanliness checks. A changed `HEAD` invalidates all gates; a dirty tree is returned as a process fact.

### Independent reviews and deterministic checks

Reviewer A completes all batches and buffers its bytes. After the candidate is reconfirmed clean and unchanged, reviewer B receives the same locator and candidate with no A output. Concurrency is allowed only when tracked project instructions explicitly declare the exact checks safe.

The orchestrator does not execute deterministic commands or inspect their output. A deterministic gate passes only when both reviewers report `qc=PASS` and `smoke=PASS` for the same candidate.

Only after both reviewers reach complete terminal outcomes does the first-pass barrier open. Findings or failed/unknown QC cause all complete batches to be relayed verbatim in reviewer and batch order. A reviewer `UNKNOWN` does not open the barrier. `transport` follows damage recovery; `environment` and `evaluation` retry the whole evaluation once in the same warm session. A second `UNKNOWN` is `needs_attention`.

### Resolution and finding closure

The orchestrator tracks IDs, never meaning.

- A fix creates a new commit and candidate; all gates restart.
- A no-commit rebuttal uses `RESPONSE` with exact `rebuts` IDs and is relayed only to originating reviewers.
- The originating reviewer closes the IDs in `PASS`, keeps them open in `FINDINGS`/`DISPUTED`, or returns `UNKNOWN`.
- A disputed finding and complete opaque exchange go once to the other-vendor reviewer for `MO_ADJUDICATION_V1`.
- `UPHOLD` returns the finding to the executor.
- `WITHDRAW` goes back to the originating reviewer, which must issue final `PASS` listing the ID in `closes`.
- `UNRESOLVED` becomes the human `unresolved_dispute` boundary.

### Agent-required E2E

E2E is skipped only when both final reviewers independently report `e2e=NA`. Any `REQUIRED` or `UNKNOWN` means it is not safely skipped.

The E2E actor receives the task locator and full candidate:

- `PASS` requires `not_run=none`, unchanged `HEAD`, and a clean tree.
- `FAIL` is relayed verbatim to the executor.
- `UNKNOWN` retries once in the same warm session, then becomes `needs_attention`.

Any subsequent commit restarts reviews, deterministic checks, and E2E.

### Verified result

The orchestrator returns one full object ID only when:

- the worktree is clean;
- reviewer A and reviewer B are different vendors;
- at least one reviewer differs in vendor from the executor;
- both reviews are final `PASS` with `open=none`, `qc=PASS`, and `smoke=PASS`;
- every introduced finding is closed or invalidated by a new candidate;
- E2E passes or both reviewers independently report `NA`;
- final `git rev-parse HEAD` equals the candidate.

## Model catalogue and self-contained build

Normal startup reads configured selections and never presents a model menu. Missing or unusable selections follow documented fallbacks while preserving vendor diversity.

- `catalog_unknown`: enumeration did not complete reliably;
- `model_missing`: enumeration succeeded and omitted the configured ID;
- `launch_failed`: the ID was listed but the interactive CLI did not reach readiness.

Claude catalogue support follows the working brain-council distribution pattern:

- authored entry: `shared/scripts/mo-models.mjs`;
- exact dev dependencies: `esbuild` `0.25.12` and `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` `0.3.191`;
- the entry statically imports the SDK and supplies `pathToClaudeCodeExecutable`;
- `tools/build-skills.mjs` bundles with `bundle: true`, `platform: node`, `format: esm`, `target: node20`, `external: []`, and no minification;
- generated outputs are byte-identical to each other at `skills/mo-herdr/scripts/mo-models.mjs` and `skills/mo-omnigent/scripts/mo-models.mjs`;
- `SHARED_PLAN` distinguishes this transformed entry from verbatim copies;
- the SDK licence is source-owned at `shared/licenses/claude-agent-sdk.LICENSE` and copied under each generated helper’s `scripts/licenses/`;
- generated helpers have no runtime SDK import or ambient `node_modules` resolution.

Isolated tests copy only the generated skill into a temporary directory without project/global `node_modules`, use a fake system Claude executable, enumerate a fixture catalogue without a conversational turn, enforce a timeout, and assert no child survives. A live fixture separately records real catalogue availability and actor launchability.

Codex continues to use `codex debug models`; OpenCode uses its native listing. No SDK is bundled without a consuming route.

The project description changes from “two dependency-free helpers” to “two self-contained runtime helpers” in `AGENTS.md`, byte-identical `CLAUDE.md`, `docs/e2e.md`, and `docs/architecture/provider-posture-script.md`. Historical verbatim framing is not rewritten.

## Project contract changes

### Version control

`mo-setup` installs this byte-identically in target `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md`:

```markdown
## Version control

Never develop directly on `main`, `master`, `develop`, or `default`. Create each
task branch from an up-to-date `develop` using `feature/<short-slug>` and use it for
the whole task.

Run the relevant checks before committing. Commit every coherent, independently
verifiable increment instead of accumulating the whole task in one commit. Use
`<type>: <what changed and why>` with `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, or
`chore`. Reference an issue or specification when one exists, but neither is
required.

End every agent-authored commit body with a blank line followed by
`Assisted-by: <harness>/<model>/<effort>`.

The final verified result is one full Git object ID. Any subsequent commit
invalidates its review and verification gates.
```

### Backlog

`docs/backlog.md` contains only unfinished, deferred, blocked, knowingly unfixed, or unsupported work. Completed entries are deleted in the same change. There is no “Closed” section.

### Glossary

`docs/glossary.md` is updated whenever implementation introduces a durable project term, changes a term’s meaning, or reveals competing terminology. One term has one meaning. Backend-neutral concepts are not defined through Herdr-specific mechanics, and Herdr-specific concepts are named as such.

The glossary is not a protocol mirror: field grammar, commands, retry policies, limits, and fixtures stay in their normative homes. Completed implementation must reconcile existing entries rather than append duplicates. Reviewers verify vocabulary consistency as part of scope review.

### Documentation

The executor updates existing knowledge only when its change makes that knowledge new or false. It does not create `CHANGELOG.md`, migration notes, explanatory reports, per-subdirectory READMEs, or routine-cleanup narration unless requested or required by a named durable consumer.

A new architecture document requires a durable boundary with no existing home. This change updates existing full-turn-retrieval and distribution decisions.

`docs/business.md` appends only original user reports and later user-authored clarifications verbatim. Derived limits, schemas, definitions, fixture choices, and implementation decisions belong in methodology, glossary, architecture, acceptance, or fixture documents.

## Error contract

| Condition | Automatic action | Terminal outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Not in Herdr or command missing | No launch | `needs_attention` with exact capability |
| Tab rename/layout failure | Retry once | `needs_attention` |
| Catalogue unavailable | Try another supported route | Attention only if diversity cannot launch |
| Configured model absent | Same-route fallback | Attention if none launches |
| Actor startup failure | One supported fallback | Attention on failure |
| Prompt stalls before state change | Retry atomic prompt once | Attention on repeat |
| `working` at interval boundary | Verify process and re-arm | Continue while healthy |
| `blocked` | Report pane location | Continue after direct unblock |
| lifecycle `unknown` | Re-arm once using metadata | Attention if persistent |
| actor/pane exits | Recreate once and restart gate | Attention on repeat |
| handoff damaged | Reissue shorter once | Gate unknown; route unsupported on repeat |
| candidate mismatch/dirty tree | Return process facts | New candidate required |
| reviewer QC/smoke fails | Finish peer first pass, relay both | Executor resolution |
| reviewer remains `UNKNOWN` | Retry once in warm session | Attention; barrier stays closed |
| review finding | Finish peer first pass, relay all | Executor resolution |
| rebuttal remains disputed | One other-vendor adjudication | Uphold, withdraw, or attention |
| E2E fails | Relay body | New candidate and all gates rerun |
| E2E unknown | Retry once | Attention on repeat |
| any new commit | Invalidate every gate | Restart candidate freeze |

